STRAWMAN_RISKS: ["Evaluating a purely sequential read of entire massive shards without an interleave step. No competent practitioner feeds 100% same-source runs into a local buffer without first interleaving multiple shards. The 'practitioner heuristic' assumes interleaving is active.", "Claiming the variance inflation can 'ONLY' be removed by scaling B with L, ignoring the existence and standard use of interleaving to break contiguous runs."]
MISSING_METRICS: ["Variance inflation as a function of interleave concurrency (cycle_length).", "Impact of variance inflation on actual optimization (does momentum smooth out transient local-correlation variance?)."]
KILLER_EXPERIMENT: Add a concurrent interleave step (C sources interleaved round-robin) before the bounded buffer. Measure variance inflation as C scales. If inflation collapses to ~1.0x at small B when C is sufficiently large, it falsifies the claim that the penalty is removed "ONLY by B growing with run-length L," proving the experiment attacked a misconfigured strawman.
RATIONALE: The claim asserts a structural ceiling that can "ONLY" be solved by scaling buffer size B against run-length L. This fundamentally ignores the primary, documented systems mechanism designed specifically to solve this issue: interleaving. By testing a sequential pipeline that reads whole shards one by one, the evaluation attacks a pathological strawman rather than a realistic practitioner setup.
